 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Noisy Correspondences:** DiNO features can produce noisy mappings where two points on the target mesh map to different points in the source NeRF [S1]. This is mitigated by using the L1 loss component of the color loss to encourage sparsity [S1].
-*   **Feature Granularity:** Using DiNO-v2 is avoided because its larger patch size results in coarser feature granularity, which is unsuitable for dense pixel-granularity correspondences [S1].
-*   **Alternative Features:** SIFT features perform poorly as they do not capture semantic similarities [S1]. Diffusion (hyper-) features double computation time without significantly improving correspondence quality [S1].
+*   **Noisy Correspondences:** DiNO features may produce noisy correspondences where points on the target mesh map to different points in the source NeRF. This is mitigated by using L1 loss for sparsity and the DoG loss [S1].
+*   **Feature Granularity:** Using coarser features (e.g., DiNO-v2) reduces correspondence quality [S1].
+*   **Semantic vs. Geometric:** The method relies on semantic similarity, not geometric similarity. Transferring appearance to semantically unrelated but similarly shaped objects (e.g., avocado to armchair) yields plausible but imperfect results [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Feature extraction and training can be completed in less than two hours on a single GPU [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Strengths:** Enables appearance transfer between semantically related objects without geometric alignment; robust semantic correspondence using ViT key-components [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Performance degrades on semantically unrelated objects, though plausible outcomes may still occur if shapes are similar [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When transferring appearance between objects or scenes with similar semantic or geometric structure [S1].
-*   **When not to use:** When high-frequency detail preservation is critical without semantic guidance, or when computational resources are extremely limited (though faster than diffusion-based alternatives) [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Training takes less than two hours on a single GPU, including feature extraction [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** High-resolution feature maps (e.g., $392 \times 392 \times 384$ for 400p images) require significant memory for storage and interpolation [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Evaluated on real-world scenes using NeRFStudio Instant-NGP and synthetic object pairs [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Enables appearance transfer between different objects/scenes; leverages semantic priors for robust correspondence [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Computationally expensive feature extraction compared to lightweight descriptors (though less so than diffusion features); performance degrades if semantic correspondence is weak [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When the goal is to transfer appearance/texture from one object to another based on semantic similarity.
+*   **When not to use:** When real-time inference is required (feature extraction is slow) or when geometric correspondence is more important than semantic appearance.
 
 ## Background
-NeRF Analogies was introduced by Michael Fischer, Zhengqin Li, Thu Nguyen-Phuoc, Aljaž Božič, Zhao Dong, Carl Marshall, and Tobias Ritschel from University College London and Meta Reality Labs [S1]. The method leverages the semantic understanding capabilities of Vision Transformers (specifically DiNO) to bridge the gap between different 3D representations [S1]. It builds on [Transformer and Vision Model Integration](13911-transformer-and-vision-model-integration.md) by utilizing ViT features for semantic correspondence [S1].
+*   **Origin:** Proposed by Michael Fischer, Zhengqin Li, Thu Nguyen-Phuoc, Aljaž Božič, Zhao Dong, Carl Marshall, and Tobias Ritschel from University College London and Meta Reality Labs [S1].
+*   **History:** Published in CVPR 2024 [S1]. The method builds on the use of Vision Transformers (ViT) for dense feature extraction and differentiable rendering [S1].
+*   **Usage:** Evaluated using NeRFStudio Instant-NGP for real-world scenes and 2D methods lifted to 3D [S1].
 
